WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.104
A model can perform differently across lighting, object size, source or population groups. The aggregate combines

00:00:07.104 --> 00:00:14.008
these results using the evaluation mixture, so a shift toward difficult cases can lower the headline

00:00:14.008 --> 00:00:17.460
score even if subgroup behaviour itself stays unchanged.

00:00:17.967 --> 00:00:24.190
These conditional accuracies are held fixed in this toy example. The model already performs much worse

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.357
on dark images.

00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:32.561
A different use environment can contain a different share of difficult conditions, even with identical behaviour

00:00:32.561 --> 00:00:33.817
inside each subgroup.

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:40.956
Can aggregate accuracy fall even when neither subgroup accuracy changes? Pause here and make a prediction

00:00:40.956 --> 00:00:41.783
before continuing.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:51.509
Aggregate accuracy is a weighted average here. A headline score can change simply because the subgroup

00:00:51.509 --> 00:00:59.580
mixture changed. For our starting inputs, Bright accuracy: 0.9. Dark accuracy: 0.5. Aggregate accuracy: 0.82.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:06.797
Compare conditional results as well as the aggregate. If subgroup performance changes too, mixture shift alone

00:01:06.797 --> 00:01:08.890
cannot explain the new result.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:15.772
A limitation report should describe which conditions failed, how many examples support it and what remains

00:01:15.772 --> 00:01:16.170
uncertain.

